Tier 1 load/store address-misaligned verification: causes 4 and 6 raised at EX, mtval = the faulting effective address, and the three commit gates that must hold ($dmem_wr_en so a misaligned store cannot corrupt memory, $rf_wr_en so a misaligned load cannot write rd, $minstret so a trapped access does not retire). Includes negative controls proving byte ops never misalign and that non-memory instructions are not caught by the check. STANDALONE - swap this in alone, do not combine with testcodes/6,7,8,9,10,11,12.
m4_define(['m4_asm_ECALL'],  ['32'b00000000000000000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_EBREAK'], ['32'b00000000000100000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_MRET'],   ['32'b00110000001000000000000001110011'])
m4_asm(ADDI,  x1,  x0,  1111100)             //   0  x1 = 124 (handler address)
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x1,  1100000101)          //   4  mtvec = 124
m4_asm(ADDI,  x3,  x0,  10101010101)         //   8  x3 = 0x555
m4_asm(SW,    x0,  x3,  100000)              //  12  word @32 = 0x00000555  [aligned SW regression]
m4_asm(ADDI,  x5,  x0,  111111111111)        //  16  x5 = -1 = 0xFFFFFFFF (destroyer payload)

   // ---- A: negative controls -- byte ops never misalign, non-memory ops are not checked ----
m4_asm(SB,    x0,  x5,  100001)              //  20  byte 33 = 0xFF, addr[1:0]=01, must NOT trap
m4_asm(LB,    x8,  x0,  100001)              //  24  x8 = sext(0xFF), must NOT trap
m4_asm(SB,    x0,  x5,  100010)              //  28  byte 34 = 0xFF, addr[1:0]=10, must NOT trap
m4_asm(LBU,   x9,  x0,  100010)              //  32  x9 = zext(0xFF), must NOT trap
m4_asm(ORI,   x28, x0,  11)                  //  36  x28 = 3. funct3=110 -> size 10 -> mask 11, and
                                             //      $addr = 0+3 = 3. Traps ONLY if the check is not
                                             //      gated on $is_load/$is_store
   // canary word @32 is now 0x00FFFF55

   // ---- B: misaligned SW -> cause 6, and the word underneath must SURVIVE ----
m4_asm(SW,    x0,  x5,  100010)              //  40  addr 34 -> TRAP 1, cause 6, mtval 34
m4_asm(ADD,   x10, x23, x0)                  //  44  x10 = mtval  of trap 1, must be 34
m4_asm(ADD,   x11, x24, x0)                  //  48  x11 = mcause of trap 1, must be 6
m4_asm(ADD,   x12, x22, x0)                  //  52  x12 = mepc   of trap 1, must be 40

   // ---- C: misaligned LW -> cause 4, and rd must keep its canary ----
m4_asm(ADDI,  x13, x0,  1000101011)          //  56  x13 = 555 canary
m4_asm(LW,    x13, x0,  100001)              //  60  addr 33 -> TRAP 2, cause 4; x13 must stay 555
m4_asm(ADD,   x14, x24, x0)                  //  64  x14 = mcause of trap 2, must be 4

   // ---- D: halfword misalign, store and load, including the funct3=101 (LHU) path ----
m4_asm(SH,    x0,  x5,  100001)              //  68  addr 33 -> TRAP 3, cause 6
m4_asm(ADD,   x15, x24, x0)                  //  72  x15 = mcause of trap 3, must be 6
m4_asm(LHU,   x13, x0,  100011)              //  76  addr 35 -> TRAP 4, cause 4; x13 STILL 555
m4_asm(ADD,   x17, x23, x0)                  //  80  x17 = mtval of trap 4, must be 35

   // ---- E: rd = x0 does not excuse the exception ----
m4_asm(LW,    x0,  x0,  100010)              //  84  addr 34 -> TRAP 5, cause 4

   // ---- F: aligned accesses still work, and the canary word is untouched ----
m4_asm(LH,    x18, x0,  100000)              //  88  addr 32, aligned -> sext(0xFF55)
m4_asm(LH,    x16, x0,  100010)              //  92  addr 34, aligned -> sext(0x00FF)
m4_asm(LW,    x6,  x0,  100000)              //  96  x6 = the canary word, must be 0x00FFFF55

   // ---- G: minstret must not count a trapped access ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x26, x0,  101100000010)        // 100  snapshot A = minstret (0xB02)
m4_asm(SW,    x0,  x5,  100010)              // 104  addr 34 -> TRAP 6, cause 6
m4_asm(CSRRS, x27, x0,  101100000010)        // 108  snapshot B = minstret
m4_asm(SUB,   x7,  x27, x26)                 // 112  x7 = delta, must be 9

m4_asm(ADDI,  x19, x0,  101010)              // 116  x19 = 42, sentinel: main path completed
m4_asm(BGE,   x0,  x0,  0)                   // 120  halt

   // ---- shared handler: snapshot mepc/mtval/mcause, count, skip the faulting instruction ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x22, x0,  1101000001)          // 124  handler: x22 = mepc
m4_asm(CSRRS, x23, x0,  1101000011)          // 128  x23 = mtval  (0x343)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x24, x0,  1101000010)          // 132  x24 = mcause (0x342)
m4_asm(ADD,   x20, x20, x24)                 // 136  x20 += mcause
m4_asm(ADDI,  x21, x21, 1)                   // 140  x21 += 1 (trap counter)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x25, x22, 100)                 // 144  x25 = mepc + 4
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x25, 1101000001)          // 148  mepc = mepc + 4
m4_asm(MRET)                                 // 152  -> PC = mepc
m4_asm(ADDI,  x30, x0,  1111100111)          // 156  poison (999): must NEVER execute
m4_asm(BGE,   x0,  x0,  0)                   // 160  handler halt (unreachable)
m4_asm_end()


All addressing uses x0 as the base register, so the immediate IS the byte address. The canary word
is dmem word 8 (byte addresses 32-35). It is built in three aligned steps -- SW 0x555, then SB 0xFF
into byte 33, then SB 0xFF into byte 34 -- giving 0x00FFFF55, a value with a distinct byte in every
lane so a wrong-lane read is visible. Every misaligned store in this test targets that same word
with payload 0xFFFFFFFF, so a broken $dmem_wr_en gate destroys the canary in a way x6 cannot miss.

Expected Waveforms:
Reg	Value		Why
x1	0000007C	124, the handler address
x3	00000555	the seed word written by the aligned SW at 12
x5	FFFFFFFF	the destroyer payload every misaligned store tries to write
x6	00FFFF55	THE memory-integrity check: the canary word after all six traps. Any misaligned store that reached the array shows up here -- a committed SW gives FFFFFFFF, a committed SH gives 00FFFFFF
x7	00000009	minstret delta across one trapped store: 1 (the snapshot at 100 itself) + 8 (the handler) + 0 (the trapping SW). A value of 10 means the trapped access was counted as a retirement
x8	FFFFFFFF	LB byte 33 = 0xFF sign-extends. Also proves a byte access at addr[1:0]=01 did not trap
x9	000000FF	LBU byte 34 = 0xFF zero-extends. Also proves addr[1:0]=10 did not trap for a byte op
x10	00000022	mtval of trap 1 = 34, the faulting effective address of the misaligned SW
x11	00000006	mcause of trap 1 = 6, store/AMO address misaligned
x12	00000028	mepc of trap 1 = 40, the address of the SW itself
x13	0000022B	555 -- canary UNCHANGED across TWO trapping loads (the LW at 60 and the LHU at 76). This is the $rf_wr_en gate. Without the canary a failed gate would be invisible, since the loaded value would also be plausible
x14	00000004	mcause of trap 2 = 4, load address misaligned. Together with x11 this is the only proof the 4-vs-6 selector is not inverted
x15	00000006	mcause of trap 3 = 6, the misaligned SH
x16	000000FF	LH at 34, aligned, upper half = 0x00FF, sign bit clear
x17	00000023	mtval of trap 4 = 35, the misaligned LHU address. LHU is funct3=101, so this is the value that proves the size decode used funct3[1:0] and not the whole field
x18	FFFFFF55	LH at 32, aligned, lower half = 0xFF55, sign bit set -> sign-extends negative
x19	0000002A	42 -- sentinel proving the main path reached the end
x20	0000001E	30 = sum of mcause over all six traps: 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 + 4 + 6
x21	00000006	THE trap count: six, in order 6, 4, 6, 4, 4, 6
x22	00000068	104 -- mepc of the LAST trap (the SW at 104)
x23	00000022	34 -- mtval of the LAST trap
x24	00000006	mcause of the LAST trap = 6
x25	0000006C	108 -- mepc + 4 from the last trap
x26	0000003C	60 -- minstret snapshot A. See the note below; this absolute value is the most fragile number in this table
x27	00000045	69 -- minstret snapshot B
x28	00000003	3 -- the ORI negative control. If this reads 0 with x21 = 7, the misalign check is not gated on $is_load/$is_store and is firing on an ALU op
x30	00000000	poison never written -- address 156 sits in MRET's 2-instruction shadow on all six handler passes

Notes:
- Detection lives at EX (@2), not MEM (@3), because both of its inputs -- $addr and $funct3 -- are
  ready there. That is what lets this exception reuse the existing $take_trap / $on_path machinery
  unchanged, and it is why the $dmem_wr_en kill arrives a full stage before the array is written.
  If any test in group B or G shows a corrupted x6, check the STAGE of the gate before the logic.

- Group A is the half of this feature that is easy to get backwards. Two separate bugs live here and
  they produce different trap counts:
    x21 = 10, x8 = x9 = 0, x6 = 00000555   the size mask is wrong for byte accesses (probably
                                           hardwired 2'b11), so all four byte ops trapped and the
                                           canary was never built
    x21 = 7,  x28 = 00000000               the check is not gated on $is_load/$is_store, so the ORI
                                           at 36 trapped on its own $addr. Note that ADDI cannot
                                           catch this -- funct3=000 maps to size 00 and mask 00, so
                                           an ungated check is invisible to every ADDI in the
                                           program. ORI (funct3=110 -> mask 11) is the only
                                           instruction here that exposes it

- x21 diagnostic table:
    x21 = 6    correct
    x21 = 5    one of the six is not firing; the wrong register names it:
               x11 != 6  -> trap 1, misaligned SW
               x14 != 4  -> trap 2, misaligned LW
               x15 != 6  -> trap 3, misaligned SH
               x17 != 23 -> trap 4, misaligned LHU (the funct3[1:0] path)
               all of the above correct -> trap 5, the LW with rd=x0. That is the "loads to x0 must
                 still take exceptions" rule; the check must not be gated on $rd_valid or $rd != 0
               x7 = 1 with everything else right -> trap 6, the group G store
    x21 = 0    $valid_misaligned_mem is not wired into $take_trap at all. Confirm with x6 =
               FFFFFFFF and x13 clobbered -- every misaligned access committed

- x7 is the only probe for the minstret gate, and it is a DELTA on purpose. The absolute values in
  x26/x27 depend on the handler being exactly 8 instructions and on nothing above them changing, so
  if you edit this program those two go stale while x7 stays valid. The arithmetic: minstret is read
  as $minstret_old, i.e. the count of instructions retired strictly BEFORE the reading instruction.
  Between the two snapshots the retirements are the snapshot at 100 itself (1) and the eight handler
  instructions (8); the trapping SW at 104, its two shadow instructions, and the two instructions in
  MRET's shadow all contribute nothing. 1 + 8 = 9.

- Group F is a regression check, not a new feature: it confirms that adding the trap did not disturb
  the aligned paths. x18 and x16 read the two halfword lanes of the same word and must differ in
  sign-extension behavior, which also confirms the LH lane select still keys on $addr[1].

- The canary word is built with two SB instructions that are NOT adjacent to each other (an LB sits
  between them), and each store is verified by the load immediately after it. That load-after-store
  distance of one instruction is the tightest this design supports without a forwarding path: the
  /dmem array is written at @3 and re-read at @3 one cycle later through $RETAIN. If x8 or x9 is
  wrong but x6 is right, suspect that timing rather than the misalign logic.

- NOT covered: access faults (causes 1/5/7 -- optional, and this memory map cannot generate them),
  misaligned accesses that CROSS a word boundary as opposed to merely sitting off-boundary (the
  check is on $addr[1:0] alone, so there is no distinct case), and mtval for causes 4/6 when the
  address is negative or above the 32-word dmem range.
